{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MuTqKrWJFLCTQ2amuvNQ71","type":"tool_result","content":"bugs:\n  - bug_id: BUG-2026-09-27T170100\n    date: \"2026-09-27\"\n    title: \"G1 accepts a mandatory requirement's satisfied_by_reference claim without evidence, before G2/G5 are known to catch a false one\"\n    severity: medium\n    priority: p2\n    scope: domain\n    what_happened: >\n      A material answer bound to a mandatory requirement passes G1 (`gateG1`, `src/domain/change/decide.ts`)\n      as soon as the requirement is named, is mandatory, and the binding is observable — G1 never reads\n      `satisfied_by_reference`. A specification report can mark a brand-new, never-implemented\n      requirement `satisfied_by_reference: true` (a claim reserved for behaviour the project already\n      honours, per the `specify` role's own instructions) and still clear G1 unchallenged.\n    what_expected: >\n      A material answer resolved only by a false `satisfied_by_reference` claim should not clear a gate\n      on the model's word alone — some control downstream must be known to catch it, or G1 itself must\n      refuse it.\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"Write a specification report whose mandatory requirement is marked `satisfied_by_reference: true` for behaviour the reference tree does not have, with a material answer bound to it.\"\n      - \"Advance the change through G1: `gateG1` names no reason and passes.\"\n      - \"Whether `preparation.ts`'s discrimination against the reference (feeding G2) or G5's evidence review would still catch the false claim before integration was not traced in this pass.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      `satisfied_by_reference` is read by `preparation.ts` (`~line 116`) to flag a requirement as\n      `undiscriminated` when the reference tree fails to exercise it, feeding a later gate's evidence —\n      but `gateG1` (`~decide.ts:505-515`) never consults it before passing the requirements document, so\n      a false claim is not refused at the gate meant to bind an answer to a real obligation; whether it\n      is refused later depends on control paths this pass did not trace to a verdict.\n    evidence: >\n      Read during the round-1 review of e01s03 (`specs/verifications/e01s03-relecture.md`, P2):\n      `gateG1`'s mandatory-answer loop names no check on `satisfied_by_reference`; `preparation.ts:116-126`\n      is the only reader found, and it is proved only by the tests that already pass, not by a new\n      failing one exercising a false claim. G2 and G5's own handling of the case was not traced.\n    reproduced: >\n      Reproduced on 2026-10-01 with a scripted agent on the reference npm target (`npm run campagne -- npm`).\n      A specification report whose only mandatory requirement asks for a function the tree does not have,\n      marked `satisfied_by_reference: true`, and an implementation that only appends a comment, pass G0 to G5\n      (unit, coverage and mutation PASS, `lastFit` absent from the candidate): `diagnoseControlCapability`\n      reports no undiscriminated requirement while the reference suite executes a test, so no failing test is\n      prepared, no arbitration is asked, and every control stays green on the reference and on the candidate.\n      The same report with `satisfied_by_reference: false` stops at \"no test can judge R1\" and waits for the\n    steps_to_reproduce:\n      - \"On an npm target that does not declare Stryker, carry a change whose frozen protocol adopts the mutation complement, so 495 installs it.\"\n      - \"Run `/495 report`: the line `495 installed N package(s) into the target …` lists `(sha512-…)` after each package.\"\n    root_cause: >\n      The risk statement is written in `src/application/report.ts` (installed_packages) for the structured report,\n      where the integrity belongs, and the text of the report shows that statement unchanged.\n    evidence: >\n      Read in review of the plain-language r